  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound`\n\n## What Is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an algorithm for finding an item in a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (linear search), it repeatedly splits the search range in half.\n\n**The intuition:** Imagine looking up a word in a physical dictionary. You don't start at page 1. You open it roughly in the middle, see if your word comes before or after the current page, and discard half the book. You repeat this until you find the word.\n\nEach step **halves** the remaining candidates:\n- 1,000 items \u2192 ~10 steps\n- 1,000,000 items \u2192 ~20 steps\n- 1,000,000,000 items \u2192 ~30 steps\n\nThis is why it's so fast: **O(log n)** time.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\n`lower_bound` finds the **index of the first element that is greater than or equal to** the target. If no such element exists (the target is larger than everything), it returns `len(items)`.\n\nThis is exactly what Python's `bis",
